latest_plan = 'Based on our reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initial Analysis:\n   a. Determine the grid dimensions.\n   b. Calculate the center point of the grid.\n   c. Compute a "scale factor" based on the grid size to adjust transformation aggressiveness.\n\n2. Structure Identification:\n   a. Identify the main structural elements (central cross, potential checkerboard patterns).\n   b. Mark these areas as "protected" with a certain probability based on their importance to the structure.\n\n3. Region Segmentation:\n   a. Use a flood-fill algorithm to identify contiguous blue regions.\n   b. Categorize regions based on size (small, medium, large) and location (edge, corner, center).\n\n4. Context Analysis:\n   a. For each region, analyze its surrounding area.\n   b. Calculate the ratio of blue to yellow cells in the vicinity.\n   c. Determine if the region is part of a larger pattern (like alternating blue-yellow).\n\n5. Transformation Scoring:\n   a. Assign a transformation score to each region based on:\n      - Size of the region\n      - Distance from center\n      - Whether it\'s part of the identified structure\n      - Context analysis results\n      - Scale factor of the grid\n   b. Adjust scores to maintain overall blue-yellow ratio appropriate for the grid size.\n\n6. Initial Transformation:\n   a. Sort regions by transformation score.\n   b. Transform regions to yellow starting from highest score, until a target ratio is reached.\n   c. Ensure symmetry by transforming corresponding regions on all four quadrants.\n\n7. Pattern Refinement:\n   a. Analyze the grid after initial transformation.\n   b. Identify areas where the pattern could be improved (e.g., isolated blue cells, breaks in structure).\n   c. Make small adjustments to enhance pattern consistency.\n\n8. Symmetry Enforcement:\n   a. Do a final pass to ensure symmetry across both vertical and horizontal axes.\n   b. Make minor adjustments if necessary, prioritizing structural integrity.\n\n9. Small Feature Preservation:\n   a. Identify any remaining small blue regions or line-like structures.\n   b. Preserve these features unless they significantly disrupt the overall pattern.\n\n10. Final Balance Check:\n    a. Calculate the final blue-to-yellow ratio.\n    b. Make minor adjustments if the ratio is significantly off from the target for the given grid size.\n\n11. Output:\n    a. Return the transformed grid.\n\nThis plan takes into account the various patterns and considerations we identified in our reflection. It allows for a more nuanced approach that can adapt to different grid sizes and maintain important structural elements while still achieving the desired transformation. The multi-step process with initial transformation and subsequent refinement should help in creating more consistent and visually appealing results that match the patterns observed in the test cases.'
